<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National People's Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as NPC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National People's Congress is the national legislature of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> and, under the constitution, the highest organ of state power. First convened in 1954, it has nearly 3,000 deputies elected indirectly for five-year terms and meets once a year; its Standing Committee legislates between sessions. In practice it approves decisions taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-peoples-congress.jpg" alt="National People's Congress" width="320" height="347" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: multiple people, e.g. Lin Huiyin and Liang Sicheng, see [2] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%B8%AD%E8%8F%AF%E4%BA%BA%E6%B0%91%E5%85%B1%E5%92%8C%E5%9C%8B%E5%9C%8B%E5%BE%BD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The People’s Republic is a party-state: the Chinese Communist Party, through its Politburo and its Standing Committee, directs the government, the army and the mass organisations, while the <b>National People’s Congress</b> serves as the formal legislature.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…anthem was booed, and that night by large crowds in more than a dozen shopping malls at 8:31 pm, a reference to 31 August 2019 and to the decision of the <b>National People's Congress</b> Standing Committee of 31 August 2014; secondary school students sang it in human chains.</blockquote><a href="/songs/glory-to-hong-kong">Glory to Hong Kong</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>National People's Congress</b> restored it on 5 March 1978 with new words naming the Communist Party, communism and Chairman Mao; after Tian's posthumous rehabilitation in 1979 his original words were restored and on 4 December 1982 it became the official national anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a></li><li><blockquote>Its success brought its composer office: Wang Shuangyin became a deputy to the <b>National People's Congress</b>, a member of the Heilongjiang provincial party committee, head of the province's model opera troupe and deputy director of its culture bureau, and was received by Jiang Qing.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sailing-the-seas-depends-on-the-helmsman">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman</a></li></ul></section>
